What is enableSimpleBroker("/topic", "/queue")?
registry.enableSimpleBroker("/topic", "/queue");

This tells Spring:

"Whenever a client subscribes to a destination starting with /topic or /queue, I (Spring's broker) will manage those subscriptions and deliver messages."

It doesn't create two different brokers.

There is one broker that manages destinations under different prefixes.

Think of it like this:

SimpleBroker
│
├── /topic
│      ├── /room/1
│      ├── /room/2
│      └── /news
│
└── /queue
       ├── /errors
       ├── /room-events
       └── /notifications
What is /topic?

/topic is one publisher → many subscribers.

Example:

Server
   │
convertAndSend("/topic/room/1")
   │
   ▼
Simple Broker
   │
   ├── Alice
   ├── Bob
   └── Charlie

Everyone receives it.

Typical uses:

Chat room
Live scores
Stock prices
News feed
What is /queue?

/queue is intended for point-to-point (single user) messaging.

Normally used together with

convertAndSendToUser(...)

Example:

messagingTemplate.convertAndSendToUser(
        "alice",
        "/queue/errors",
        "Invalid token"
);

Only Alice receives it.

The frontend subscribes to

/user/queue/errors

Spring internally maps it to Alice's session.

Typical uses:

Errors
Notifications
Room events
Friend requests
"You were kicked"
Why is it called queue?

Historically, in messaging systems:

Topic

means

One message

↓

Many receivers

while

Queue

means

One message

↓

One receiver

Spring follows the same terminology.

